#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e29a91-79e6-5a56-bdce-42cc0e768f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-2\\cdot \\cos t}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642578} {:answer/id #uuid "77dbee76-ccdd-5a32-886e-535b648b691f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642581} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5a5b40-d90e-5113-a929-c6976ea2e620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+2\\cdot \\cos t}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642584} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4f5701-65dc-57de-8229-b95141aeded7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-\\cos t}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642587} {:answer/id #uuid "fd557e8d-9ce2-5e34-87b7-6bf44fd9632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77dbee76-ccdd-5a32-886e-535b648b691f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642581} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db60454-2ca6-54f9-8b1a-b4f4433d1fd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2198e1d-7f37-59e5-83be-d6b8c3a9e2e8" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}-2\\cdot \\cot t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\cot t=\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}-2\\cdot \\cot t \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}+\\frac{-2\\cdot \\cos t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t}+\\frac{-2\\cdot (\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t-2\\cdot \\cos t+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity. Then factor the numerator and cancel the common linear factor, retaining the original denominator exclusions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot (\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)-2\\cdot \\cos t-\\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3-2\\cdot \\cos t-\\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot (1-\\cos t)+(\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{(1-\\cos t)(3+\\cos t)}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $\\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}$, on the original domain $\\sin t\\ne0, 1-\\cos t\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5821db7a-52c1-5685-ac1f-a24dea525453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657666} {:answer/id #uuid "896f4fb3-780c-5394-a7c4-2c48f10ada9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657673} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a0983b-55bc-545c-b0a0-d6ac4873d0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657678} {:answer/id #uuid "e78062d7-2943-58e7-8f8a-56e43f8a7b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657681} {:answer/id #uuid "ff390e11-93f5-5e2b-b5a0-81c8b17df467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff390e11-93f5-5e2b-b5a0-81c8b17df467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657683} :answer-field/id #uuid "74026f16-a351-5b82-a094-4d783b5f71be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90db644a-fd4a-5c58-b43a-e0452a6336d1" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+3.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f4d5cf-a461-500b-bfff-59db9cb0a932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73f4d5cf-a461-500b-bfff-59db9cb0a932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657669} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d327192-03f4-5c34-804d-23703a9d60c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99c047a7-758b-5db9-9256-347c7b790ef5" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-6}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-6.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3441c0fe-baf2-58ec-be43-0bc6b1dc52bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657663} {:answer/id #uuid "38fa11e4-1ab5-5a61-9543-a49af6fcfcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657665} {:answer/id #uuid "7194ccdf-1cf5-5662-86a2-61c314c00de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657668} {:answer/id #uuid "8179b0e5-d27d-5436-849a-0b6a81884973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657671} {:answer/id #uuid "d8efcb49-91d1-50ec-ad3e-649069f99598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38fa11e4-1ab5-5a61-9543-a49af6fcfcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657665} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d105e46-25ee-5153-a38b-ae7c0422f899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab67789f-4616-5914-aa8d-59736a54c4f6" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-2x+12}}.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2x+12.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-2x+12{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35aba803-7e19-5f64-ae12-70549369c0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(8x+2)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35aba803-7e19-5f64-ae12-70549369c0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(8x+2)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657664} :answer-field/id #uuid "114eabe9-88a9-521f-991b-55f2cc647709" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e61f263e-2780-5a15-b73e-edd6546f8ecb" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8x+2}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8x+2.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(8x+2)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ad8cba-4c43-5466-991c-8f58f1b2c414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657657} {:answer/id #uuid "31e7578d-8b52-580a-afa3-98f20764e7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657662} {:answer/id #uuid "6da4c05a-424b-5485-beff-b0b4d9364431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657667} {:answer/id #uuid "856b17f1-3e5d-5e34-99a4-069fedec4af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657672} {:answer/id #uuid "96693fb7-81ed-5735-a956-5196bd2f34a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e7578d-8b52-580a-afa3-98f20764e7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657662} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bdd7f6-dc11-5437-83b6-6f0871573543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f48647ea-8ebd-5583-b713-aef1cac5a291" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-6-7x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-6-7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-6-7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e5001b-c3ed-5873-aede-ffa85ff51f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e5001b-c3ed-5873-aede-ffa85ff51f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660467} :answer-field/id #uuid "992b741e-55ff-5161-b382-5112fabfb8f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f971c7dd-8fff-5af0-b192-0b8235cd5523" :question/math-academy-id "q-317311" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨0,2,7⟩,$ $b=⟨-3,1,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨5,-2,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\,i-\\,((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\,j+\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\,k \\\\  & =8\\,i+20\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f3e99d-e3d7-5097-a2cb-ec250767a45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4f3e99d-e3d7-5097-a2cb-ec250767a45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669860} :answer-field/id #uuid "515a3092-9d3a-53ff-b134-90e57859f26f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669918} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e23bc3b-0767-5b04-83d9-b44821712f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e23bc3b-0767-5b04-83d9-b44821712f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669719} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddcd2f23-2ed7-5bd5-97d2-5278390026c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "020fab38-ab05-5b07-9d6a-f39c5439fdb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321978" :question/problem "![](images/53/53aa9f55c84bab63cc1bbfcc396d617287f8a2c7740c9749521289b22237a622.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the lower semicircle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle.\n\n![](images/e4/e43fa60cee504db4449c575aa0a01f7b157c1774db0f88939c1f98192716891e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the required lower semicircle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the lower semicircle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2615a0d3-b908-5da6-8a94-3f1b0298f0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin\\theta+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2615a0d3-b908-5da6-8a94-3f1b0298f0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin\\theta+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669515} :answer-field/id #uuid "d100fd19-5a14-5912-902b-9471e2082f47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bfe8a0-9623-54c8-9554-2cfabc5e6a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49bfe8a0-9623-54c8-9554-2cfabc5e6a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669581} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9e8dd5-7a06-5c09-b21e-cdc06f5479cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ab1fc3c-9d41-50cb-8d00-5be64d82fded" :question/math-academy-id "q-321941" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6de586fe8fe643862433f8597a6937fc05893c1ba5b141c358afdbaae7b72c70.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,7)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,7).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(3,7).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +7,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "621863a5-0270-5a64-8c33-16a67b1ad882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "621863a5-0270-5a64-8c33-16a67b1ad882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669620} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfad52e6-992a-5c69-a7ed-41ff61880463" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f117a9c3-6bf2-5260-a7f4-e1a7a2f9b54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f117a9c3-6bf2-5260-a7f4-e1a7a2f9b54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669854} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdabeae0-4a8c-55f9-a4f4-a5f82a334d69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eb0d834-05b6-5d1d-90c0-a8fd50dca594" :question/math-academy-id "q-321832" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6a2c51fec19e92910a4723e6ceeb63312ab1082f7aff5eee49c1ef55e56c83c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=7.$ Substituting $r=7$ gives\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e29230-0a4e-5cff-ab94-00881ba6c1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e29230-0a4e-5cff-ab94-00881ba6c1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669508} :answer-field/id #uuid "150e39bf-aaa1-5236-9afe-53edb0216abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e74b5eea-2e1e-54d1-88c6-f0ed7048ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e74b5eea-2e1e-54d1-88c6-f0ed7048ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669831} :answer-field/id #uuid "da1f1e0d-6be6-5a01-9ba8-15705b714cf6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "261353e7-f95f-5044-967a-e8610fc4fbb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-321958" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a619839e28cb0caaba42366b5a5a573b348e8b53a213c37319d7eb56dc5e859.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=8.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/8a/8ab3cf3e55a699da8fd5cd552d58a2f61ccd8acd96c2161e1d0a1bb7b79800ac.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f127ce1-69bb-50ac-80e2-de9770dda3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f127ce1-69bb-50ac-80e2-de9770dda3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669506} :answer-field/id #uuid "4099f852-502b-5a66-8b49-a7c1a448fd8b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f5b20a-1a6b-56e6-acdd-7ece3973d27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f5b20a-1a6b-56e6-acdd-7ece3973d27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669764} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae78c283-0a99-5327-adef-5ab1e810c1be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3933fdc7-73fa-5daa-99e7-1a128b2fd99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-274766" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f73702f7b4408148fe51e3361f32cfc791d0fc5dae45c39380f81d567d4fe397.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta$ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/9d/9de42c7154740e93f944661bdefc916044af581264ec2af0db9af624e7680777.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67276a49-a63f-5a1c-9321-47c92bd8ef2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669628} {:answer/id #uuid "7e582545-3ae8-54a4-88f4-918a97293385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669667} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecef37f-6f84-5a27-b731-5ca7e72351b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669721} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ca32f2-2877-551c-ab1c-e8d585229057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669763} {:answer/id #uuid "fc10b036-ebf0-5df3-b4a1-a16c584f3a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ca32f2-2877-551c-ab1c-e8d585229057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669763} :answer-field/id #uuid "93569b45-6cea-569a-8fbf-d8bd9f82ae62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d4ebbba-539a-58a5-9b60-0ef7ff0d0a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-106296" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =25(1) \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556d1c5a-d38c-5163-9301-c2836659073f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669607} {:answer/id #uuid "84d3ba51-3b48-59f7-b4f6-0f398f7fd8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669676} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2bb683-c917-588f-a371-da56946b1c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669691} {:answer/id #uuid "b6162f79-45e8-57da-9f0b-e93be2554e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669758} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4790aa-1818-5789-a8b7-718bb64905fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556d1c5a-d38c-5163-9301-c2836659073f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669607} :answer-field/id #uuid "7376b615-6b75-59b8-8651-84dfa458b259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "706e6bf5-8f0d-5c3f-a0ae-76088f4d59d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106292" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b64ef6f76519e424e27b14eeaa6d090fa3a0fc94ab8381e5a36a7fc2fbe0a74.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $1$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f8485a-76ec-52d0-bf22-112619c92238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669461} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3f0bf5-82a3-5707-9715-bcd43b3e9c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669502} {:answer/id #uuid "295ace62-151c-50e8-843f-dfe356cad0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669522} {:answer/id #uuid "7f981505-2c97-5f88-a315-67ddc3b3b201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669668} {:answer/id #uuid "e17f683d-8bd4-5c7f-97ff-4eb15eb26710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f981505-2c97-5f88-a315-67ddc3b3b201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669668} :answer-field/id #uuid "62cba062-7281-518d-99ea-c9fa55df49f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "757fbf31-a64a-5a54-9064-1c9196ed9d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-79204" :question/problem "![](images/61/61ebe29c5b9a45ea98b1137b0cde94adff7e5d43b3da4e89fc5cab938770db13.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/19/19288384020e643b0335f7aaaea8875839b5e33ba69594ac75a5ee1fd63301a0.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8179270-7fbb-56d4-98d5-d1545eea46cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8179270-7fbb-56d4-98d5-d1545eea46cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669784} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f205096-e696-518b-bfd6-f3e12d196bec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dce79830-1a6c-56be-a85a-f31cb5a73036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dce79830-1a6c-56be-a85a-f31cb5a73036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669820} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfdd1b86-2035-5ff0-ade0-ddb472f1f9f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c78fdb72-df80-5a4d-8f3f-f824f21a4ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-321892" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d98b8895d8b6322729c25533584e3ef686cfd068f05363f2afd9f207d539a70a.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $13$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(13,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=13.$ Substituting $r=13$ gives\n\n$$\nx=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d917aa7-b076-57a2-ae2f-ee791da3a14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669664} {:answer/id #uuid "961e90ec-b2ac-5872-a8b6-09ff7cf7403f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669710} {:answer/id #uuid "a391e222-18ef-5744-9213-0a085ac0d45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669736} {:answer/id #uuid "bad59b50-ed0d-5bd0-895b-56183aa4fba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669765} {:answer/id #uuid "eda350ba-30da-5baf-82ac-172d18258600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "961e90ec-b2ac-5872-a8b6-09ff7cf7403f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669710} :answer-field/id #uuid "708aa4cd-61ac-508d-894a-88dae27934ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee1a6a0f-53e9-52c1-aee7-58d54ed5429c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106298" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1911a5a7-f3f7-570f-9fa3-c1981c4207d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25(-1-2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674225} {:answer/id #uuid "2af3b741-0e3b-5d69-b4c9-aeec185861b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6(-1-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674228} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee743cc-f41a-5f09-bc26-34dd01d784e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10(-1-2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674231} {:answer/id #uuid "506242d7-6946-5793-af48-24427370b9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(-1-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674234} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3a5ea2-bbee-5c81-a219-c37346ebec71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18(-1-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af3b741-0e3b-5d69-b4c9-aeec185861b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6(-1-2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674228} :answer-field/id #uuid "697835dc-03ad-5fb5-88bf-35beb7f43aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41c093aa-391c-5ebf-acae-bdc80fe54491" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-1-2x)^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{3},\\,g=-1-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3g^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1-2x)^{2} \\\\  &=-6(-1-2x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f7b679-b110-593d-9a4d-48c344350489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(-9+3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f7b679-b110-593d-9a4d-48c344350489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(-9+3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674230} :answer-field/id #uuid "b127e32c-07a6-53ec-8d86-716356179dde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4488b435-75c4-5bf8-836f-8c025405b71b" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-9+3x)^{2}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=-9+3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=6(-9+3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "358cdd35-f54f-5f0a-96f8-8aa8b44b8ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21(8-5x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674229} {:answer/id #uuid "685dc9ff-3893-53af-a4fe-684701e82263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8(8-5x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674236} {:answer/id #uuid "771e8765-6d6b-5ea7-b38e-82d4e23649c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6(8-5x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674238} {:answer/id #uuid "a07bb43c-0d8b-56c9-bf60-bcb8516d2494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(8-5x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674242} {:answer/id #uuid "b5258c1e-66c7-5a68-9de6-0e14c158d25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20(8-5x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5258c1e-66c7-5a68-9de6-0e14c158d25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20(8-5x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674245} :answer-field/id #uuid "7917f945-dbcb-5a17-9d25-fe021a3c403f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c07f75d-1f18-51f4-b433-e46bd0df0550" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(8-5x)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=8-5x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (8-5x)^{3} \\\\  &=-20(8-5x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832f82b0-8996-58d2-aa20-2768c526e9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(5+3x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "832f82b0-8996-58d2-aa20-2768c526e9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(5+3x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674240} :answer-field/id #uuid "de68a7e9-8973-5b5b-b7b3-263757a2e394" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a25c900d-1561-5094-9272-47bf64ff1c06" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(5+3x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=5+3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=12(5+3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afff144-faa8-5d98-8b52-2e0fcc9a7f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(-7+6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674226} {:answer/id #uuid "1c35b387-63bd-565d-b5dc-feba286107ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(-7+6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674227} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff15c7e-b33c-59b4-b72a-86eca43164f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(-7+6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674241} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e08094-b4f8-524a-b559-837c1a01dfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(-7+6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674248} {:answer/id #uuid "f771748e-0724-5882-9ec8-fdf9ce712c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(-7+6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1afff144-faa8-5d98-8b52-2e0fcc9a7f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(-7+6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674226} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb94c143-3dce-569c-b7cf-c17c32239041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c83476a7-334e-550f-a570-977c18f19b5f" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-7+6x)^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{5},\\,g=-7+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=5g^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=5\\cdot 6\\cdot (-7+6x)^{4} \\\\  &=30(-7+6x)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255f9156-287e-5cc9-b8ed-eb31b15e83ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "255f9156-287e-5cc9-b8ed-eb31b15e83ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674307} :answer-field/id #uuid "b024a65a-2e57-571c-a34d-025276659956" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a831727-2211-5532-89c4-e5a4ccb9ae4a" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-7x+16}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-7x+16.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{-7(0)+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "767fa440-8fc1-5358-9025-7c8125e6aaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "767fa440-8fc1-5358-9025-7c8125e6aaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674312} :answer-field/id #uuid "099a5827-9c3f-5cf3-bff5-003f736749ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf444c8-3239-54ca-9516-8df227c41a5f" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}$ at the point where $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2{x}^{2}+6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x+6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+3)}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(3)+6}{2\\sqrt{2(3{)}^{2}+6(3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c11f32b-69c1-5fd0-bddb-c652f4dd99dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c11f32b-69c1-5fd0-bddb-c652f4dd99dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674313} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3399efe-ec83-56d2-bfb3-9ba448fc95b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "309f479d-646b-5563-8225-cb5faadfd4a1" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}$ at the point where $x=4$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4{x}^{2}-7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (8x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(4)-7}{2\\sqrt{4(4{)}^{2}-7(4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15b33e8-c7f6-5a5e-93f2-cb68037c61a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e15b33e8-c7f6-5a5e-93f2-cb68037c61a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674321} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0342523-f1be-5f79-85fa-6dd2455256a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86491180-1d5e-5901-bd2b-dafda235930e" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5{x}^{2}+9x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9-10x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (9-10x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10(1)}{2\\sqrt{-5(1{)}^{2}+9(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3af54b83-704e-55d5-a89d-14d9c5f3ff86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674310} {:answer/id #uuid "482d0285-39be-5f4b-8137-eea61ed52b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{39}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674311} {:answer/id #uuid "80cc6b4d-e037-5552-9468-63b241864f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674314} {:answer/id #uuid "e031eaae-76f9-58af-b3e6-50abf25c826b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674320} {:answer/id #uuid "e938f3d1-5a47-5d48-988a-c15b0f05149d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e031eaae-76f9-58af-b3e6-50abf25c826b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674320} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28412ef-227e-5ded-9964-e41d96174b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8ccbb82-8efb-534c-acab-7f6468bd8bed" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{37+7x}$ at the point where $x=-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=37+7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-4} & ={(\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}})|}_{x=-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7(-4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8454e2-fd06-5b12-a953-9a328f913f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8454e2-fd06-5b12-a953-9a328f913f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682452} :answer-field/id #uuid "321b6fb9-9a04-5137-a245-8a44b5f13049" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20498caa-b8e8-5b95-8861-4c51c52bcab8" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4{x}^{2}-4.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (8x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d47820-4029-569d-99d0-e0be394de5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x+6}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d47820-4029-569d-99d0-e0be394de5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x+6}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682446} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd451c6-7e49-5554-9e59-95c653380e37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3951e5c2-6316-5b76-8773-67b41f65d8fc" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=7{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=14x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (14x+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(7x+6)}{2\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x+6}{\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}+12x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339b6b7d-143b-5ba1-8722-4d2137011ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339b6b7d-143b-5ba1-8722-4d2137011ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682444} :answer-field/id #uuid "8879e90a-0ab9-5448-811b-1d06d58f847b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186682447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8898456e-d243-5557-b6fe-0a41725d56cb" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-7x-4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-7x-4.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
